Ladybird’s text-shadow layers shadow the text’s decoration lines as
well as its glyphs, and the lines of an outer decorating box are painted
below those of the boxes nested in it. Blink does the same: Its
TextDecorationPainter paints the lines in TextShadowPaintPhase::kShadow
too. So does WebKit, whose TextDecorationPainter paints the underline
and overline once for each of its m_shadow layers. And Gecko’s
nsTextFrame::PaintOneShadow() draws the decorations along with the run,
in the shadow’s color. Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5 all
shadow the underline, overline, and line-through, blurred or not, and
the lines a decorating ancestor propagates to a shadowed span. But none
of them shadows the line-through that a ::selection adds with the
originating text’s own shadow.
